  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1235 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Models indicate that the Pacific NW upper\nlow will persist over WA and OR through Sat, then begin to open\nand dig south towards southern OR and northern CA on Sun. The \nupper high to the east changes very little, remaining amplified \nover the central US. So, the end result seems to be very little \nchange over the weekend, with isolated to scattered convection \neach afternoon and evening over the higher terrain and portions \nof the plains, with the focus areas along the southern tier. \nTemps remain somewhat stagnant as well, with each day climbing \ninto the 80s for the high valleys and 90s for the plains, which \ncontinues to be above normal.\n\nMonday through Thursday...Long range model solutions start to \ndiverge, though the operational GFS is leaning more along the \nlines of the NBM. The Pacific NW low pressure system opens up \nand pinwheels to the northeast across the northern Rockies on \nMon, sweeping an upper shortwave across the Great Basin and \nRocky Mt region. The upper trough remains over the Pacific NW \nthough, dropping another low center down along the West Coast \nand keeping deep southwest flow aloft across the Four Corners. \nThe upper disturbance Mon will tap into available moisture to \nspawn increased activity, then Tue onward the deep SW flow aloft\nwill greatly enhance monsoonal moisture advection into CO. All \nthis to say - precipitation chances increase in coverage across \nthe entire forecast area starting Mon, with the best chances \nacross the higher terrain. Increased storm coverage and in turn \nintensity could mean growing flash flood potential for area burn\nscars, so though monitoring is done on a daily basis, those \nareas will need more attention starting Mon. As for temps, the \nextended procedure keeps highs at a steady state through the \nweek, with temps each day above normal. Given the expected \nincreased precip and cloud cover, high temps for the mts and \nhigh valleys will likely cool a few degrees, but there is still \ntime to fine tune that. Moore",
